Title: gaze: hard-fail when the review worktree cannot be created; one gate per PR at a time
Created: 2026-09-02
Author: claude-fable

--- log ---
2026-09-02T11:53Z claude-fable created

2026-09-02T11:55Z handoff sections written: worktree-fallback silent continuation + missing per-PR gate lock, per search-works-for-zotero PR #184 incident
--- body ---
## Context

Observed 2026-09-02 in `search-works-for-zotero`, PR #184, ticket 0489. A
team lead ran `/gaze` on its own PR. The skill's worktree-creation step
(`skills/gaze/SKILL.md`, Phase 1 "Isolation setup", around lines 89-135 —
`git worktree add "$primary_root/.claude/worktrees/review-<pr-number>"
origin/"$PR_BRANCH"`) could not create the review worktree under the
session's isolation guard, and the run fell back into the lead's own
worktree instead of failing. A second `/gaze` fork on the same PR did the
same thing independently.

Consequences within 13 minutes, entirely from that one silent fallback:

- 11:27:42Z — a genuine APPROVED gate comment posted.
- 11:36:41Z — a second fork, running in the same non-isolated tree and with
  no way to know a prior gate existed, read the APPROVED comment as
  fabricated, pushed a "correction" commit, and posted a REROLL comment.
- A third comment from the branch owner, disputing the REROLL.
- A round-2 panel that escalated for human arbitration.
- One reviewer observed another actor's uncommitted log line appear
  mid-check in the shared tree — direct evidence the isolation boundary was
  gone, not merely bypassed once.

Every seat agreed the underlying code was correct; only the review *record*
was broken. Cost: roughly two hours of agent time plus one human
arbitration, for a defect whose root cause is one un-checked shell command.

Two distinct mechanisms are in play, and the fix needs both:

1. **The worktree-creation step has no failure path.** `git worktree add`
   at SKILL.md's Phase 1 is invoked bare — no `|| die`, no check that the
   directory exists afterward. When creation fails, execution continues
   into whatever tree the process happens to be standing in, silently
   discarding the isolation the skill's own prose calls "the isolation
   boundary" (SKILL.md, Phase 1 preamble: "The worktree is the isolation
   boundary; no main-repo checkout is ever needed").
2. **A gate's identity is illegible to a later gate on the same PR.**
   Nothing records, in a place a second `/gaze` invocation would check
   before starting, that a gate is already live or already ruled on that PR.
   A second fork has no way to distinguish "a legitimate prior verdict
   exists" from "a comment was fabricated" — both look identical from
   inside a shared, non-isolated tree.

Same family, one layer up: harness ticket 0551 documents a fork mistaking
itself for the coordinator after a `SendMessage` resume — a different
symptom of an agent losing track of its own identity/authority relative to
a peer process. This ticket is the /gaze-specific, worktree-isolation
instance of that broader class.

Relevant memory already saved by the orchestrating session:
`projects/-home-haduong-CNRS-code-search-works-for-zotero/memory/feedback_one_gate_per_pr_at_a_time.md`.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — Phase 1 "Isolation setup" (the bare `git
  worktree add` with no failure check, ~lines 89-135); Phase 6 verdict
  comment template (search "verify-gate verdict", ~line 86 references the
  `## /verify-gate verdict` block format); § Fork liveness / window-expiry
  recovery logic (~lines 92-107), which already reasons about competing
  markers on a PR and is the natural home for a gate-lock check.
- `rules/workflow.md` § Subagents — orchestration guidance for fan-out and
  fork behavior; needs one line establishing "one gate per PR at a time."
- `skills/verify-gate/` (phase 6 invocation target) — the verdict producer;
  its comment template is what would carry the proposed identity fields.
- `~/.claude/scripts/pretooluse-worktree-path-guard.sh` — the isolation
  guard whose denial is what triggered the silent fallback in the observed
  incident; understanding exactly when it denies `git worktree add` is
  prerequisite to writing the red-first test for Action (a).
- `projects/-home-haduong-CNRS-code-search-works-for-zotero/memory/feedback_one_gate_per_pr_at_a_time.md`
  — the orchestrator's own record of this incident (search-works-for-zotero
  side; read for the full timeline, not duplicated here beyond the facts
  above).
- Harness ticket 0551 — the general "fork mistakes itself for the
  coordinator" defect this ticket specializes.

## Actions

This ticket proposes; it does not implement. A follow-on ticket (or this one
reopened for implementation) does the actual SKILL.md edit.

1. **Hard-fail on worktree-creation failure.** In `skills/gaze/SKILL.md`
   Phase 1, change the bare `git worktree add …` line to check its exit
   status and the resulting directory's existence; on failure, `/gaze`
   reports a named error (e.g. "gaze: cannot create isolated review
   worktree for PR <n>; refusing to fall back into the invoking tree") and
   stops. Never silently continue in the invoking tree.
2. **Per-PR gate lock.** Before Phase 6 (and ideally before Phase 1, to
   fail fast), `/gaze` checks for a live gate marker on the PR — a comment
   header carrying the gate's session id and start time, or a lock file
   under the primary repo's `.claude/worktrees/` namespace. If a live gate
   is found, refuse to start a second gate and report the holder (session
   id, start time) instead of proceeding.
3. **Legible gate identity in the verdict comment.** The Phase 6
   `## /verify-gate verdict` comment template gains identity fields: gate
   session id, review worktree path, and the tip SHA the verdict was ruled
   on. A later gate (or a human) can then tell a legitimate prior verdict
   from a suspected fabrication by checking these against the PR's actual
   state, instead of having no way to check at all.
4. **One-line orchestration rule.** `rules/workflow.md` § Subagents gains:
   "One gate per PR at a time: an orchestrator never launches its own
   `/gaze` on a branch while the lead that owns it is still live." This is
   the same standing as the existing subagent bullets in that section
   (fork/coordinator confusion, uncommitted shared-tree writes) — process
   discipline stated once, for every future orchestrator to read.

## Test

Red-first, per `coding-python.md`'s testing conventions (source inspection,
not subprocess `--help`, since this is prose/shell-script content rather
than a Python module):

1. A test that simulates a denied `git worktree add` (or, at minimum,
   inspects `skills/gaze/SKILL.md`'s Phase 1 block via source read) and
   asserts the block contains an explicit failure check — not a bare
   `git worktree add` line with no exit-status handling immediately after
   it. Confirm this test is RED against the current SKILL.md before any fix
   lands (the whole point: today's text has no such check).
2. A test asserting the Phase 6 verdict-comment template (wherever it is
   defined — SKILL.md prose or a template file under `skills/gaze/` or
   `skills/verify-gate/`) names the three identity fields: gate session id,
   review worktree path, tip SHA. Confirm RED against the current template
   before any fix lands.

## Verification

- [ ] Test 1 is red against current `skills/gaze/SKILL.md`, green after
      Action (a) lands.
- [ ] Test 2 is red against the current verdict-comment template, green
      after Action (c) lands.
- [ ] `rules/workflow.md` § Subagents contains the new one-line rule after
      Action (d) lands, and `make check` (or this repo's equivalent lint
      gate) still passes.
- [ ] A manual or scripted trace confirms: with the isolation guard denying
      worktree creation, `/gaze` now stops with the named error instead of
      continuing in the invoking tree.

## Invariants

- `/gaze`'s existing two-round REROLL cap, circuit-breaker abort paths, and
  window-expiry recovery logic (SKILL.md § Fork liveness) must keep working
  unchanged — this ticket adds a new failure path, it does not touch the
  existing ones.
- No change here should require every gate to serialize globally across
  unrelated PRs — the lock is per-PR, not a global mutex.
- Existing SKILL.md tests (adherence, structure) stay green.

## Exit criteria

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` Phase 1 hard-fails (named error, no fallback) when
  the review worktree cannot be created.
- A per-PR gate lock exists and is checked before a second `/gaze` gate
  starts on a PR that already has a live one.
- The Phase 6 verdict comment carries gate session id, review worktree
  path, and ruled tip SHA.
- `rules/workflow.md` § Subagents states the one-gate-per-PR-at-a-time rule.
- Both red-first tests above exist, were confirmed red pre-fix, and are
  green post-fix.
- `erg check` passes; the repo's fast check gate passes.
